<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayat tribe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bayat are a Turkic tribe of the Oghuz confederation, listed among its tribes by Mahmud al-Kashgari in the 11th century. Bayat groups moved west with the Seljuk and later migrations into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> they were one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> tribes. Bayat khans ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maku">Maku</a> in north-western Iran from the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayat-tribe.jpg" alt="Bayat tribe" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruzgar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Turkic tradition traces the tribe to Bayat, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-khan">Oghuz Khan</a>: in the Genealogy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> by Abu al-Ghazi Bahadur, Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>, Bayat was the second son of Gun Khan, the first son of Oghuz Khan, and the name means rich. The Oghuz-nameh in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>' al-tawarikh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> renders it as rich, full of grace. With the Kayi, the Bayat stood at the head of all 24 Oghuz tribes, and an old Turkmen proverb says that the Kayi and Bayat shall lead the people. Chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> of the 10th to 12th centuries record Bayats among the Oghuz tribes there under the name bouts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Oghuz began to move from the Aral steppes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> between the 11th and 13th centuries, the Bayat spread through the region, their first destination being the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>. After Mongol attacks in the 13th century they divided. One group went east and north-east, which is why the surname Bayat is still found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. A second went south-west towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, and the name is prominent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arak-iran">Arak</a>, Hamedan, Malayer, Zanjan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. A third went north-west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, where part turned west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and part south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><p>In western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> their main areas of settlement were the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afyonkarahisar">Afyon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikesir">Balıkesir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a>, and the name survives in thirty-two mainly western Anatolian place names. Until 1928 the settlement of Gyzyletrek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> was called Bayathaji.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Bayat are Muslim and form sub-ethnic groups of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> and Azerbaijanis. They speak a southern dialect of Azerbaijani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, their own dialect of Turkish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and the Ersari dialect of Turkmen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Bayat Turkmens live in several districts of Lebap province in Turkmenistan and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> province of Uzbekistan, especially Olat and Qarako'l. Bayat is a common surname in Iran and Azerbaijan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayat (tribe)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maku, a town built under a cliff in the far north-west of Iran near Mount Ararat, came under the hereditary khans of the Turkic <b>Bayat</b> clan after the death of Nader Shah in 1747.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-maku">Khanate of Maku</a></li></ul></section>
